4. ⚙️ Components Description
4.1 Terraform CLI

Terraform CLI is used to execute Infrastructure as Code operations.

Responsibilities:
Initialize Terraform environment
Validate Terraform configuration
Generate execution plan
Provision Azure resources
4.2 AzureRM Provider

AzureRM provider acts as a bridge between Terraform and Microsoft Azure APIs.

Responsibilities:
Authenticate with Azure
Create and manage Azure resources
Handle Terraform resource provisioning
4.3 Resource Groups

Azure Resource Groups are logical containers used to organize Azure resources.

Features:
Resource isolation
Centralized management
Environment segregation
4.4 Azure Storage Accounts

Azure Storage Accounts provide scalable and secure cloud storage.

Features:
Blob storage
High availability
Data redundancy
Cloud-native storage services
5. 📂 Terraform Configuration Structure
File Name	Purpose
provider.tf	Azure provider configuration
main.tf	Resource creation logic
variables.tf	Variable definitions
terraform.tfvars	Variable values
output.tf	Terraform outputs
6. 🔄 Workflow
Step 1 — Initialize Terraform
terraform init

Downloads required providers and initializes Terraform.

Step 2 — Validate Configuration
terraform validate

Checks Terraform syntax and configuration validity.

Step 3 — Generate Execution Plan
terraform plan

Displays resources that will be created or modified.

Step 4 — Deploy Infrastructure
terraform apply

Creates Azure Resource Groups and Storage Accounts.

7. 🔁 Dynamic Resource Creation using for_each

The project uses Terraform for_each to dynamically create multiple Azure resources.

Benefits:
Reusable code
Reduced duplication
Scalable deployments
Simplified infrastructure management

Example:

for_each = var.rg-name

Terraform automatically iterates through all map objects.

